/*This is a block comment that ends at the bottom of this file.
Comments are not executable.
Evetyhing that is not a comment is code that is compiled.

Top-right pane is the watch window.
Variables can be set there to view data during execution.
Just type in the value on the selected watch window.
"ctrl+e" edits the highlighted watch pane name.
"delete" will delete the highlighted watch pane row.

Bottom-left pane is the disassembler.
Bottom-right pane is the call stack.

"ctrl+alt+c" compiles the code.
Compiling code means converting from a programming language
into executable instructions. Executable instructions are 
are shown as assembly instructions in the pane below.

"ctrl+f7" toggles the status bar on the bottom of the text op.
Status bar shows file_name, line_number, text_messages

In debug mode, breakpoints can be set with 
the mouse on the left or from the cursor line with f9.
Breakpoints will not stop on commented lines.

f5 will run/continue the compiled code.
Execution will pause if it reaches a breakpoint.
"shift+f5" will stop execution.
"ctrl+shift+f5" will restart execution.
Always compile before execution.
f10 will step over a line of code in top-left text pane.
f10 will run a single assembly instruction in bottom-left disassembly pane.
f11 will debug step into a function in top-left text pane.
shift+f11 will debug step out of a function.

ctrl+alt+o toggles text op messages in debug mode.
ctrl+alt+i toggles disassembly in debug mode.

Every program requires a main function.
Main function declaration is 
main :: ()
Everything within the curly braces {  } represents the scope of that function.
Execution always starts at the main function.

